(; constants used by Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.LinearIndependent.Defs, declared with their translated types ;)

(; a constant applied as a function: DFunLike.coe ;)
Finsupp__linearCombination__app : (a1 : Set) -> (a2 : (Elem I -> M)) -> (a3 : Elem (Finsupp I R)) -> Elem M.

(; a constant applied as a function: DFunLike.coe ;)
LinearEquiv__symm__app : (a1 : Elem (LinearEquiv R R (RingHom__id R) (RingHom__id R) (Finsupp I R) (Subtype M (x6 => Membership__mem M (Submodule R M) (Submodule__span R M (Set__range M I v)) x6)))) -> (a2 : Elem (Subtype M (x7 => Membership__mem M (Submodule R M) (Submodule__span R M (Set__range M I v)) x7))) -> Elem (Finsupp I R).

(; a constant applied as a function: DFunLike.coe ;)
LinearIndependent__linearCombinationEquiv__app : (a1 : Proof (LinearIndependent I R M v)) -> (a2 : Elem (Finsupp I R)) -> Elem (Subtype M (x5 => Membership__mem M (Submodule R M) (Submodule__span R M (Set__range M I v)) x5)).

(; a constant applied as a function: DFunLike.coe ;)
LinearIndependent__repr__app : (a1 : Proof (LinearIndependent I R M v)) -> (a2 : Elem (Subtype M (x4 => Membership__mem M (Submodule R M) (Submodule__span R M (Set__range M I v)) x4))) -> Elem (Finsupp I R).

(; doc: An additive commutative group is an additive group with commutative `(+)`.  [Wikidata Q181296](https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q181296) ;)
(; //LEAN:AddCommGroup ;)
AddCommGroup : (G : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: An additive commutative monoid is an additive monoid with commutative `(+)`. ;)
(; //LEAN:AddCommMonoid ;)
AddCommMonoid : (M : Set) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:AddHom.mk ;)
AddHom__mk : (M : Set) -> (N : Set) -> (toFun : (Elem M -> N)) -> (map_add' : Proof (forall M (x2 => forall M (y => Eqt N (toFun (plus x2 y)) (plus (toFun x2) (toFun y)))))) -> Elem (AddHom M N).

(; doc: The underlying function ;)
(; //LEAN:AddHom.toFun ;)
AddHom__toFun : (M : Set) -> (N : Set) -> (self : Elem (AddHom M N)) -> (x2 : Elem M) -> Elem N.

(; doc: The bot (`⊥`, `\bot`) element  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `⊥` in identifiers is `bot`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Bot.bot ;)
Bot__bot : (X : Set) -> Elem X.

(; doc: An ordered additive monoid is `CanonicallyOrderedAdd` if the ordering coincides with the subtractibility relation, which is to say, `a ≤ b` iff there exists `c` with `b = a + c`. This is satisfied by the natural numbers, for example, but not the integers or other nontrivial ordered groups.  We have `a ≤ b + a` and `a ≤ a + b` as separate fields. In the commutative case the second field is redundant, but in the noncommutative case (satisfied most relevantly by the ordinals), this extra field allows us to prove more things without the extra commutativity assumption. ;)
(; //LEAN:CanonicallyOrderedAdd ;)
CanonicallyOrderedAdd : (X : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: Set / lattice complement  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `ᶜ` in identifiers is `compl`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Compl.compl ;)
Compl__compl : (X : Set) -> (x : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; doc: Two elements of a lattice are disjoint if their inf is the bottom element.   (This generalizes disjoint sets, viewed as members of the subset lattice.)  Note that we define this without reference to `⊓`, as this allows us to talk about orders where the infimum is not unique, or where implementing `Inf` would require additional `Decidable` arguments. ;)
(; //LEAN:Disjoint ;)
Disjoint : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> Prop.

(; doc: A `DivisionRing` is a `Ring` with multiplicative inverses for nonzero elements.  An instance of `DivisionRing K` includes maps `ratCast : ℚ → K` and `qsmul : ℚ → K → K`. Those two fields are needed to implement the `DivisionRing K → Algebra ℚ K` instance since we need to control the specific definitions for some special cases of `K` (in particular `K = ℚ` itself). See also note [forgetful inheritance]. Similarly, there are maps `nnratCast ℚ≥0 → K` and `nnqsmul : ℚ≥0 → K → K` to implement the `DivisionSemiring K → Algebra ℚ≥0 K` instance.  If the division ring has positive characteristic `p`, our division by zero convention forces `ratCast (1 / p) = 1 / 0 = 0`. ;)
(; //LEAN:DivisionRing ;)
DivisionRing : (K : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: `∅` or `{}` is the empty set or empty collection. It is supported by the `EmptyCollection` typeclass.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `{}` in identifiers is `empty`.   * The recommended spelling of `∅` in identifiers is `empty`. ;)
(; //LEAN:EmptyCollection.emptyCollection ;)
EmptyCollection__emptyCollection : (X : Set) -> Elem X.

(; doc: If `h : α = β` is a proof of type equality, then `h.mp : α → β` is the induced "cast" operation, mapping elements of `α` to elements of `β`.  You can prove theorems about the resulting element by induction on `h`, since `rfl.mp` is definitionally the identity function. ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.mp ;)
Eq__mp : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (h : Proof (Eqt Set X Y)) -> (a : Elem X) -> Elem Y.

(; doc: If `h : α = β` is a proof of type equality, then `h.mpr : β → α` is the induced "cast" operation in the reverse direction, mapping elements of `β` to elements of `α`.  You can prove theorems about the resulting element by induction on `h`, since `rfl.mpr` is definitionally the identity function. ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.mpr ;)
Eq__mpr : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (h : Proof (Eqt Set X Y)) -> (b : Elem Y) -> Elem X.

(; doc: `Eq.refl a : a = a` is reflexivity, the unique constructor of the equality type. See also `rfl`, which is usually used instead. ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.refl ;)
Eq__refl : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt X a a).

(; doc: Equality is symmetric: if `a = b` then `b = a`.  Because this is in the `Eq` namespace, if you have a variable `h : a = b`, `h.symm` can be used as shorthand for `Eq.symm h` as a proof of `b = a`.  For more information: [Equality](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/quantifiers_and_equality.html#equality) ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.symm ;)
Eq__symm : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (Eqt X a b)) -> Proof (Eqt X b a).

(; doc: Equality is transitive: if `a = b` and `b = c` then `a = c`.  Because this is in the `Eq` namespace, if you have variables or expressions `h₁ : a = b` and `h₂ : b = c`, you can use `h₁.trans h₂ : a = c` as shorthand for `Eq.trans h₁ h₂`.  For more information: [Equality](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/quantifiers_and_equality.html#equality) ;)
(; //LEAN:Eq.trans ;)
Eq__trans : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> (c : Elem X) -> (h : Proof (Eqt X a b)) -> (h1 : Proof (Eqt X b c)) -> Proof (Eqt X a c).

(; doc: `α ≃ β` is the type of functions from `α → β` with a two-sided inverse. ;)
(; //LEAN:Equiv ;)
Equiv : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> Set.

(; doc: If a function `f` is injective on a set `s`, then `s` is equivalent to `f '' s`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Equiv.Set.imageOfInjOn ;)
Equiv__Set__imageOfInjOn : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> (H : Proof (Set__InjOn X Y f s)) -> Elem (Equiv (Set__Elem X s) (Set__Elem Y (Set__image X Y f s))).

(; doc: `univ α` is equivalent to `α`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Equiv.Set.univ ;)
Equiv__Set__univ : (X : Set) -> Elem (Equiv (Set__Elem X (Set__univ X)) X).

(; doc: The backward map of an equivalence.  Do NOT use `e.invFun` directly. Use the coercion of `e.symm` instead. ;)
(; //LEAN:Equiv.invFun ;)
Equiv__invFun : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (self : Elem (Equiv X Y)) -> (x : Elem Y) -> Elem X.

(; doc: If `f : α → β` is an injective function, then domain `α` is equivalent to the range of `f`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Equiv.ofInjective ;)
Equiv__ofInjective : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (hf : Proof (Function__Injective X Y f)) -> Elem (Equiv X (Set__Elem Y (Set__range Y X f))).

(; doc: Composition of equivalences `e₁ : α ≃ β` and `e₂ : β ≃ γ`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Equiv.trans ;)
Equiv__trans : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (Z : Set) -> (x : Elem (Equiv X Y)) -> (x1 : Elem (Equiv Y Z)) -> Elem (Equiv X Z).

(; doc: `Finset α` is the type of finite sets of elements of `α`. It is implemented as a multiset (a list up to permutation) which has no duplicate elements. ;)
(; //LEAN:Finset ;)
Finset : (X : Set) -> Set.

(; doc: `∑ x ∈ s, f x` is the sum of `f x` as `x` ranges over the elements of the finite set `s`.  When the index type is a `Fintype`, the notation `∑ x, f x`, is a shorthand for `∑ x ∈ Finset.univ, f x`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Finset.sum ;)
Finset__sum : (I : Set) -> (M : Set) -> (s : Elem (Finset I)) -> (f : (Elem I -> M)) -> Elem M.

(; doc: `univ` is the universal finite set of type `Finset α` implied from the assumption `Fintype α`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Finset.univ ;)
Finset__univ : (X : Set) -> Elem (Finset X).

(; doc: `Finsupp α M`, denoted `α →₀ M`, is the type of functions `f : α → M` such that `f x = 0` for all but finitely many `x`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Finsupp ;)
Finsupp : (X : Set) -> (M : Set) -> Set.

(; doc: Given `f : α ≃ β`, we can map `l : α →₀ M` to `equivMapDomain f l : β →₀ M` (computably) by mapping the support forwards and the function backwards. ;)
(; //LEAN:Finsupp.equivMapDomain ;)
Finsupp__equivMapDomain : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (M : Set) -> (f : Elem (Equiv X Y)) -> (l : Elem (Finsupp X M)) -> Elem (Finsupp Y M).

(; doc: Interprets (l : α →₀ R) as a linear combination of the elements in the family (v : α → M) and evaluates this linear combination. ;)
(; //LEAN:Finsupp.linearCombination ;)
Finsupp__linearCombination : (X : Set) -> (M : Set) -> (R : Set) -> (v : (Elem X -> M)) -> Elem (LinearMap R R (RingHom__id R) (Finsupp X R) M).

(; doc: `Finsupp.linearCombinationOn M v s` interprets `p : α →₀ R` as a linear combination of a subset of the vectors in `v`, mapping it to the span of those vectors.  The subset is indicated by a set `s : Set α` of indices. ;)
(; //LEAN:Finsupp.linearCombinationOn ;)
Finsupp__linearCombinationOn : (X : Set) -> (M : Set) -> (R : Set) -> (v : (Elem X -> M)) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> Elem (LinearMap R R (RingHom__id R) (Subtype (Finsupp X R) (x3 => Membership__mem (Finsupp X R) (Submodule R (Finsupp X R)) (Finsupp__supported X R R s) x3)) (Subtype M (x4 => Membership__mem M (Submodule R M) (Submodule__span R M (Set__image X M v s)) x4))).

(; //LEAN:Finsupp.module ;)
Finsupp__module : (X : Set) -> (M : Set) -> (R : Set) -> Proof (Module R (Finsupp X M)).

(; doc: `single a b` is the finitely supported function with value `b` at `a` and zero otherwise. ;)
(; //LEAN:Finsupp.single ;)
Finsupp__single : (X : Set) -> (M : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem M) -> Elem (Finsupp X M).

(; doc: `sum f g` is the sum of `g a (f a)` over the support of `f`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Finsupp.sum ;)
Finsupp__sum : (X : Set) -> (M : Set) -> (N : Set) -> (f : Elem (Finsupp X M)) -> (g : (Elem X -> Elem M -> N)) -> Elem N.

(; doc: The support of a finitely supported function (aka `Finsupp`). ;)
(; //LEAN:Finsupp.support ;)
Finsupp__support : (X : Set) -> (M : Set) -> (self : Elem (Finsupp X M)) -> Elem (Finset X).

(; doc: `Finsupp.supported M R s` is the `R`-submodule of all `p : α →₀ M` such that `p.support ⊆ s`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Finsupp.supported ;)
Finsupp__supported : (X : Set) -> (M : Set) -> (R : Set) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> Elem (Submodule R (Finsupp X M)).

(; doc: `Fintype α` means that `α` is finite, i.e. there are only finitely many distinct elements of type `α`. The evidence of this is a finset `elems` (a list up to permutation without duplicates), together with a proof that everything of type `α` is in the list. ;)
(; //LEAN:Fintype ;)
Fintype : (X : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: `Fintype.linearCombination R v f` is the linear combination of vectors in `v` with weights in `f`. This variant of `Finsupp.linearCombination` is defined on fintype indexed vectors.  This map is linear in `v` if `R` is commutative, and always linear in `f`. See note [bundled maps over different rings] for why separate `R` and `S` semirings are used. ;)
(; //LEAN:Fintype.linearCombination ;)
Fintype__linearCombination : (X : Set) -> (M : Set) -> (R : Set) -> (v : (Elem X -> M)) -> Elem (LinearMap R R (RingHom__id R) (Elem X -> R) M).

(; doc: A function `f : α → β` is called injective if `f x = f y` implies `x = y`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Function.Injective ;)
Function__Injective : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> Prop.

(; doc: A function `f : α → β` is called surjective if every `b : β` is equal to `f a` for some `a : α`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Function.Surjective ;)
Function__Surjective : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> Prop.

(; doc: Function composition, usually written with the infix operator `∘`. A new function is created from two existing functions, where one function's output is used as input to the other.  Examples:  * `Function.comp List.reverse (List.drop 2) [3, 2, 4, 1] = [1, 4]`  * `(List.reverse ∘ List.drop 2) [3, 2, 4, 1] = [1, 4]`  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `∘` in identifiers is `comp`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Function.comp ;)
Function__comp : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (W : Set) -> (f : (Elem Y -> W)) -> (g : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (x : Elem X) -> Elem W.

(; //LEAN:Function.injective_of_subsingleton ;)
Function__injective_of_subsingleton : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Subsingleton X)) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> Proof (Function__Injective X Y f).

(; doc: `a • b` computes the product of `a` and `b`. The meaning of this notation is type-dependent, but it is intended to be used for left actions.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `•` in identifiers is `smul`. ;)
(; //LEAN:HSMul.hSMul ;)
HSMul__hSMul : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (Z : Set) -> (x : Elem X) -> (x1 : Elem Y) -> Elem Z.

(; //LEAN:Iff.rfl ;)
Iff__rfl : (a : Prop) -> Proof (iff a a).

(; //LEAN:Iff.symm ;)
Iff__symm : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> (h : Proof (iff a b)) -> Proof (iff b a).

(; //LEAN:Iff.trans ;)
Iff__trans : (a : Prop) -> (b : Prop) -> (c : Prop) -> (h : Proof (iff a b)) -> (h1 : Proof (iff b c)) -> Proof (iff a c).

(; doc: `insert x xs` inserts the element `x` into the collection `xs`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Insert.insert ;)
Insert__insert : (X : Set) -> (Z : Set) -> (x : Elem X) -> (x1 : Elem Z) -> Elem Z.

(; doc: A mixin for cancellative addition. ;)
(; //LEAN:IsCancelAdd ;)
IsCancelAdd : (G : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: A domain is a nontrivial semiring such that multiplication by a nonzero element is cancellative on both sides. In other words, a nontrivial semiring `R` satisfying `∀ {a b c : R}, a ≠ 0 → a * b = a * c → b = c` and `∀ {a b c : R}, b ≠ 0 → a * b = c * b → a = c`.  This is implemented as a mixin for `Semiring α`. To obtain an integral domain use `[CommRing α] [IsDomain α]`. ;)
(; //LEAN:IsDomain ;)
IsDomain : (X : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: `IsEmpty α` expresses that `α` is empty. ;)
(; //LEAN:IsEmpty ;)
IsEmpty : (X : Set) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:Lean.PrettyPrinter.Delaborator.Delab ;)
Lean__PrettyPrinter__Delaborator__Delab : Set.

(; doc: A linear equivalence is an invertible linear map. ;)
(; //LEAN:LinearEquiv ;)
LinearEquiv : (R : Set) -> (S : Set) -> (x2 : Elem (RingHom R S)) -> (x3 : Elem (RingHom S R)) -> (M : Set) -> (X : Set) -> Set.

(; //LEAN:LinearEquiv.apply_symm_apply ;)
LinearEquiv__apply_symm_apply : (R : Set) -> (S : Set) -> (M : Set) -> (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Semiring R)) -> (_ : Proof (Semiring S)) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommMonoid M)) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommMonoid X)) -> (module_M : Proof (Module R M)) -> (x4 : Proof (Module S X)) -> (x5 : Elem (RingHom R S)) -> (x6 : Elem (RingHom S R)) -> (h : Proof (RingHomInvPair R S x5 x6)) -> (h1 : Proof (RingHomInvPair S R x6 x5)) -> (e : Elem (LinearEquiv R S x5 x6 M X)) -> (c : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt X (e (LinearEquiv__symm__app e c)) c).

(; //LEAN:LinearEquiv.ofBijective._proof_1 ;)
LinearEquiv__ofBijective___proof_1 : (R : Set) -> (X : Set) -> (M : Set) -> (X1 : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Semiring R)) -> (_ : Proof (Semiring X)) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommMonoid M)) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommMonoid X1)) -> (module_M : Proof (Module R M)) -> (x4 : Proof (Module X X1)) -> (x5 : Elem (RingHom R X)) -> (f : Elem (LinearMap R X x5 M X1)) -> (hf : Proof (Function__Bijective M X1 f)) -> Proof (Function__Injective M X1 f).

(; //LEAN:LinearEquiv.ofBijective._proof_2 ;)
LinearEquiv__ofBijective___proof_2 : (R : Set) -> (X : Set) -> (M : Set) -> (X1 : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Semiring R)) -> (_ : Proof (Semiring X)) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommMonoid M)) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommMonoid X1)) -> (module_M : Proof (Module R M)) -> (x4 : Proof (Module X X1)) -> (x5 : Elem (RingHom R X)) -> (x6 : Elem (RingHom X R)) -> (f : Elem (LinearMap R X x5 M X1)) -> (_ : Proof (RingHomInvPair R X x5 x6)) -> (hf : Proof (Function__Bijective M X1 f)) -> Proof (Eqt (Submodule X X1) (LinearMap__range R X M X1 x5 f) (Top__top (Submodule X X1))).

(; doc: An `Injective` linear map `f : M →ₗ[R] M₂` defines a linear equivalence between `M` and `f.range`. See also `LinearMap.ofLeftInverse`. ;)
(; //LEAN:LinearEquiv.ofInjective ;)
LinearEquiv__ofInjective : (R : Set) -> (X : Set) -> (M : Set) -> (X1 : Set) -> (module_M : Proof (Module R M)) -> (x4 : Proof (Module X X1)) -> (x5 : Elem (RingHom R X)) -> (x6 : Elem (RingHom X R)) -> (f : Elem (LinearMap R X x5 M X1)) -> (h2 : Proof (Function__Injective M X1 f)) -> Elem (LinearEquiv R X x5 x6 M (Subtype X1 (x7 => Membership__mem X1 (Submodule X X1) (LinearMap__range R X M X1 x5 f) x7))).

(; doc: The top submodule of `M` is linearly equivalent to `M`. ;)
(; //LEAN:LinearEquiv.ofTop ;)
LinearEquiv__ofTop : (R : Set) -> (M : Set) -> (module_M : Proof (Module R M)) -> (p : Elem (Submodule R M)) -> (h : Proof (Eqt (Submodule R M) p (Top__top (Submodule R M)))) -> Elem (LinearEquiv R R (RingHom__id R) (RingHom__id R) (Subtype M (x2 => Membership__mem M (Submodule R M) p x2)) M).

(; doc: Linear equivalences are symmetric. ;)
(; //LEAN:LinearEquiv.symm ;)
LinearEquiv__symm : (R : Set) -> (S : Set) -> (M : Set) -> (X : Set) -> (module_M : Proof (Module R M)) -> (x4 : Proof (Module S X)) -> (x5 : Elem (RingHom R S)) -> (x6 : Elem (RingHom S R)) -> (h : Proof (RingHomInvPair R S x5 x6)) -> (h1 : Proof (RingHomInvPair S R x6 x5)) -> (e : Elem (LinearEquiv R S x5 x6 M X)) -> Elem (LinearEquiv S R x6 x5 X M).

(; doc: The equivalence of types underlying a linear equivalence. ;)
(; //LEAN:LinearEquiv.toEquiv ;)
LinearEquiv__toEquiv : (R : Set) -> (S : Set) -> (M : Set) -> (X : Set) -> (modM : Proof (Module R M)) -> (x4 : Proof (Module S X)) -> (x5 : Elem (RingHom R S)) -> (x6 : Elem (RingHom S R)) -> (e : Elem (LinearEquiv R S x5 x6 M X)) -> Elem (Equiv M X).

(; doc: The linear map underlying a linear equivalence. ;)
(; //LEAN:LinearEquiv.toLinearMap ;)
LinearEquiv__toLinearMap : (R : Set) -> (S : Set) -> (x2 : Elem (RingHom R S)) -> (x3 : Elem (RingHom S R)) -> (M : Set) -> (X : Set) -> (self : Elem (LinearEquiv R S x2 x3 M X)) -> Elem (LinearMap R S x2 M X).

(; //LEAN:LinearIndependent.linearCombinationEquiv._proof_1 ;)
LinearIndependent__linearCombinationEquiv___proof_1 : (I : Set) -> (R : Set) -> (M : Set) -> (v : (Elem I -> M)) -> (_ : Proof (Semiring R)) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommMonoid M)) -> (_ : Proof (Module R M)) -> (c : Elem (Finsupp I R)) -> Proof (Membership__mem M (Submodule R M) (Submodule__span R M (Set__range M I v)) (Finsupp__linearCombination__app R v c)).

(; //LEAN:LinearIndependent.linearCombinationEquiv._proof_2 ;)
LinearIndependent__linearCombinationEquiv___proof_2 : (I : Set) -> (R : Set) -> (M : Set) -> (v : (Elem I -> M)) -> (_ : Proof (Semiring R)) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommMonoid M)) -> (_ : Proof (Module R M)) -> (hv : Proof (LinearIndependent I R M v)) -> Proof (and (Function__Injective (Finsupp I R) (Subtype M (x3 => Membership__mem M (Submodule R M) (Submodule__span R M (Set__range M I v)) x3)) (LinearMap__codRestrict R R (Finsupp I R) M (RingHom__id R) (Submodule__span R M (Set__range M I v)) (Finsupp__linearCombination I M R v) (LinearIndependent__linearCombinationEquiv___proof_1 I R M v))) (Function__Surjective (Finsupp I R) (Subtype M (x4 => Membership__mem M (Submodule R M) (Submodule__span R M (Set__range M I v)) x4)) (LinearMap__codRestrict R R (Finsupp I R) M (RingHom__id R) (Submodule__span R M (Set__range M I v)) (Finsupp__linearCombination I M R v) (LinearIndependent__linearCombinationEquiv___proof_1 I R M v)))).

(; doc: A map `f` between an `R`-module and an `S`-module over a ring homomorphism `σ : R →+* S` is semilinear if it satisfies the two properties `f (x + y) = f x + f y` and `f (c • x) = (σ c) • f x`. Elements of `LinearMap σ M M₂` (available under the notation `M →ₛₗ[σ] M₂`) are bundled versions of such maps. For plain linear maps (i.e. for which `σ = RingHom.id R`), the notation `M →ₗ[R] M₂` is available. An unbundled version of plain linear maps is available with the predicate `IsLinearMap`, but it should be avoided most of the time. ;)
(; //LEAN:LinearMap ;)
LinearMap : (R : Set) -> (S : Set) -> (x2 : Elem (RingHom R S)) -> (M : Set) -> (X : Set) -> Set.

(; doc: A linear map `f : M₂ → M` whose values lie in a submodule `p ⊆ M` can be restricted to a linear map M₂ → p.  See also `LinearMap.codLift`. ;)
(; //LEAN:LinearMap.codRestrict ;)
LinearMap__codRestrict : (R : Set) -> (X : Set) -> (M : Set) -> (X1 : Set) -> (x6 : Elem (RingHom R X)) -> (p : Elem (Submodule X X1)) -> (f : Elem (LinearMap R X x6 M X1)) -> (h : Proof (forall M (c => Membership__mem X1 (Submodule X X1) p (f c)))) -> Elem (LinearMap R X x6 M (Subtype X1 (x7 => Membership__mem X1 (Submodule X X1) p x7))).

(; doc: Composition of two linear maps is a linear map ;)
(; //LEAN:LinearMap.comp ;)
LinearMap__comp : (X : Set) -> (X1 : Set) -> (X2 : Set) -> (X3 : Set) -> (X4 : Set) -> (X5 : Set) -> (x6 : Proof (Module X X3)) -> (x7 : Proof (Module X1 X4)) -> (x8 : Proof (Module X2 X5)) -> (x9 : Elem (RingHom X X1)) -> (x10 : Elem (RingHom X1 X2)) -> (x11 : Elem (RingHom X X2)) -> (f : Elem (LinearMap X1 X2 x10 X4 X5)) -> (g : Elem (LinearMap X X1 x9 X3 X4)) -> Elem (LinearMap X X2 x11 X3 X5).

(; //LEAN:LinearMap.ext ;)
LinearMap__ext : (R : Set) -> (S : Set) -> (M : Set) -> (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Semiring R)) -> (_ : Proof (Semiring S)) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommMonoid M)) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommMonoid X)) -> (_ : Proof (Module R M)) -> (_ : Proof (Module S X)) -> (x6 : Elem (RingHom R S)) -> (f : Elem (LinearMap R S x6 M X)) -> (g : Elem (LinearMap R S x6 M X)) -> (h : Proof (forall M (x7 => Eqt X (f x7) (g x7)))) -> Proof (Eqt (LinearMap R S x6 M X) f g).

(; //LEAN:LinearMap.injOn_of_disjoint_ker ;)
LinearMap__injOn_of_disjoint_ker : (R : Set) -> (X : Set) -> (M : Set) -> (X1 : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Ring R)) -> (_ : Proof (Ring X)) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup M)) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup X1)) -> (_ : Proof (Module R M)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X X1)) -> (x6 : Elem (RingHom R X)) -> (f : Elem (LinearMap R X x6 M X1)) -> (p : Elem (Submodule R M)) -> (s : Elem (powerset M)) -> (h : Proof (Set__Subset M s (SetLike__coe (Submodule R M) M p))) -> (hd : Proof (Disjoint (Submodule R M) p (LinearMap__ker R X M X1 x6 f))) -> Proof (Set__InjOn M X1 f s).

(; doc: The kernel of a linear map `f : M → M₂` is defined to be `comap f ⊥`. This is equivalent to the set of `x : M` such that `f x = 0`. The kernel is a submodule of `M`. ;)
(; //LEAN:LinearMap.ker ;)
LinearMap__ker : (R : Set) -> (X : Set) -> (M : Set) -> (X1 : Set) -> (x6 : Elem (RingHom R X)) -> (f : Elem (LinearMap R X x6 M X1)) -> Elem (Submodule R M).

(; //LEAN:LinearMap.ker_eq_bot ;)
LinearMap__ker_eq_bot : (R : Set) -> (X : Set) -> (M : Set) -> (X1 : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Ring R)) -> (_ : Proof (Ring X)) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup M)) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommGroup X1)) -> (_ : Proof (Module R M)) -> (_ : Proof (Module X X1)) -> (x6 : Elem (RingHom R X)) -> (f : Elem (LinearMap R X x6 M X1)) -> Proof (iff (Eqt (Submodule R M) (LinearMap__ker R X M X1 x6 f) (Bot__bot (Submodule R M))) (Function__Injective M X1 f)).

(; //LEAN:LinearMap.mk ;)
LinearMap__mk : (R : Set) -> (S : Set) -> (x2 : Elem (RingHom R S)) -> (M : Set) -> (X : Set) -> (toAddHom : Elem (AddHom M X)) -> (map_smul' : Proof (forall R (m => forall M (x7 => Eqt X (AddHom__toFun M X toAddHom (HSMul__hSMul R M M m x7)) (HSMul__hSMul S X X (x2 m) (AddHom__toFun M X toAddHom x7)))))) -> Elem (LinearMap R S x2 M X).

(; doc: The range of a linear map `f : M → M₂` is a submodule of `M₂`. See Note [range copy pattern]. ;)
(; //LEAN:LinearMap.range ;)
LinearMap__range : (R : Set) -> (X : Set) -> (M : Set) -> (X1 : Set) -> (x6 : Elem (RingHom R X)) -> (f : Elem (LinearMap R X x6 M X1)) -> Elem (Submodule X X1).

(; doc: A linear order is reflexive, transitive, antisymmetric and total relation `≤`. We assume that every linear ordered type has decidable `(≤)`, `(<)`, and `(=)`. ;)
(; //LEAN:LinearOrder ;)
LinearOrder : (X : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: The membership relation `a ∈ s : Prop` where `a : α`, `s : γ`.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `∈` in identifiers is `mem`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Membership.mem ;)
Membership__mem : (X : Set) -> (Z : Set) -> (x : Elem Z) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> Prop.

(; doc: A module is a generalization of vector spaces to a scalar semiring. It consists of a scalar semiring `R` and an additive monoid of "vectors" `M`, connected by a "scalar multiplication" operation `r • x : M` (where `r : R` and `x : M`) with some natural associativity and distributivity axioms similar to those on a ring. ;)
(; //LEAN:Module ;)
Module : (R : Set) -> (M : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: An `R`-module `M` is torsion-free if scalar multiplication by an element `r : R` is injective if multiplication (on `R`) by `r` is.  For domains, this is equivalent to the usual condition of `r • m = 0 → r = 0 ∨ m = 0`. See `smul_eq_zero`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Module.IsTorsionFree ;)
Module__IsTorsionFree : (R : Set) -> (M : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: `-a` computes the negative or opposite of `a`. The meaning of this notation is type-dependent.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `-` in identifiers is `neg` (when used as a unary operator). ;)
(; //LEAN:Neg.neg ;)
Neg__neg : (X : Set) -> (x : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; doc: A `Ring` is a `Semiring` with negation making it an additive group. ;)
(; //LEAN:Ring ;)
Ring : (R : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: The identity ring homomorphism from a semiring to itself. ;)
(; //LEAN:RingHom.id ;)
RingHom__id : (X : Set) -> Elem (RingHom X X).

(; //LEAN:RingHomInvPair.ids ;)
RingHomInvPair__ids : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Semiring X)) -> Proof (RingHomInvPair X X (RingHom__id X) (RingHom__id X)).

(; doc: `a \ b` is the set difference of `a` and `b`, consisting of all elements in `a` that are not in `b`.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `\` in identifiers is `sdiff`. ;)
(; //LEAN:SDiff.sdiff ;)
SDiff__sdiff : (X : Set) -> (x : Elem X) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; doc: A `Semiring` is a type with addition, multiplication, a `0` and a `1` where addition is commutative and associative, multiplication is associative and left and right distributive over addition, and `0` and `1` are additive and multiplicative identities. ;)
(; //LEAN:Semiring ;)
Semiring : (X : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: Given the set `s`, `Elem s` is the `Type` of element of `s`.  It is currently an abbreviation so that instance coming from `Subtype` are available. If you're interested in making it a `def`, as it probably should be, you'll then need to create additional instances (and possibly prove lemmas about them). See e.g. `Mathlib/Data/Set/Order.lean`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.Elem ;)
Set__Elem : (X : Set) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> Set.

(; doc: `f` is injective on `s` if the restriction of `f` to `s` is injective. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.InjOn ;)
Set__InjOn : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> Prop.

(; doc: The subset relation on sets. `s ⊆ t` means that all elements of `s` are elements of `t`.  Note that you should **not** use this definition directly, but instead write `s ⊆ t`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.Subset ;)
Set__Subset : (X : Set) -> (x : Elem (powerset X)) -> (x1 : Elem (powerset X)) -> Prop.

(; //LEAN:Set.compl_eq_univ_sdiff ;)
Set__compl_eq_univ_sdiff : (X : Set) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> Proof (Eqt (powerset X) (Compl__compl (powerset X) s) (SDiff__sdiff (powerset X) (Set__univ X) s)).

(; doc: Restrict domain of a function `f` to a set `s`. Same as `Subtype.restrict` but this version takes an argument `↥s` instead of `Subtype s`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.domRestrict ;)
Set__domRestrict : (X : Set) -> (x : (Elem X -> Set)) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> (f : ((a1 : Elem X) -> x a1)) -> (a : Elem (Set__Elem X s)) -> Elem (x (Subtype__val X (x1 => Membership__mem X (powerset X) s x1) a)).

(; doc: The image of `s : Set α` by `f : α → β`, written `f '' s`, is the set of `b : β` such that `f a = b` for some `a ∈ s`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.image ;)
Set__image : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> Elem (powerset Y).

(; //LEAN:Set.image_eq_range ;)
Set__image_eq_range : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> Proof (Eqt (powerset Y) (Set__image X Y f s) (Set__range Y (Set__Elem X s) (x => f (Subtype__val X (x1 => Membership__mem X (powerset X) s x1) x)))).

(; doc: `inclusion` is the "identity" function between two subsets `s` and `t`, where `s ⊆ t` ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.inclusion ;)
Set__inclusion : (X : Set) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> (t : Elem (powerset X)) -> (h : Proof (Set__Subset X s t)) -> (x : Elem (Set__Elem X s)) -> Elem (Set__Elem X t).

(; //LEAN:Set.inclusion_injective ;)
Set__inclusion_injective : (X : Set) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> (t : Elem (powerset X)) -> (h : Proof (Set__Subset X s t)) -> Proof (Function__Injective (Set__Elem X s) (Set__Elem X t) (Set__inclusion X s t h)).

(; //LEAN:Set.injOn_iff_injective ;)
Set__injOn_iff_injective : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> Proof (iff (Set__InjOn X Y f s) (Function__Injective (Set__Elem X s) Y (Set__domRestrict X (a => Y) s f))).

(; //LEAN:Set.isEmpty_coe_sort ;)
Set__isEmpty_coe_sort : (X : Set) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> Proof (iff (IsEmpty (Set__Elem X s)) (Eqt (powerset X) s (EmptyCollection__emptyCollection (powerset X)))).

(; doc: Range of a function.  This function is more flexible than `f '' univ`, as the image requires that the domain is in Type and not an arbitrary Sort. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.range ;)
Set__range : (X : Set) -> (I : Set) -> (f : (Elem I -> X)) -> Elem (powerset X).

(; //LEAN:Set.subset_univ ;)
Set__subset_univ : (X : Set) -> (s : Elem (powerset X)) -> Proof (Set__Subset X s (Set__univ X)).

(; doc: The universal set on a type `α` is the set containing all elements of `α`.  This is conceptually the "same as" `α` (in set theory, it is actually the same), but type theory makes the distinction that `α` is a type while `Set.univ` is a term of type `Set α`. `Set.univ` can itself be coerced to a type `↥Set.univ` which is in bijection with (but distinct from) `α`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Set.univ ;)
Set__univ : (X : Set) -> Elem (powerset X).

(; doc: The coercion from a term of a `SetLike` to its corresponding `Set`. ;)
(; //LEAN:SetLike.coe ;)
SetLike__coe : (A : Set) -> (B : Set) -> (x : Elem A) -> Elem (powerset B).

(; doc: `singleton x` is a collection with the single element `x` (notation: `{x}`).  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `{x}` in identifiers is `singleton`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Singleton.singleton ;)
Singleton__singleton : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (x : Elem X) -> Elem Y.

(; doc: Pick some representation of `x : span R w` as a linear combination in `w`, `((Finsupp.mem_span_iff_linearCombination _ _ _).mp x.2).choose` ;)
(; //LEAN:Span.repr ;)
Span__repr : (R : Set) -> (M : Set) -> (w : Elem (powerset M)) -> (x3 : Elem (Subtype M (x4 => Membership__mem M (Submodule R M) (Submodule__span R M w) x4))) -> Elem (Finsupp (Set__Elem M w) R).

(; doc: A submodule of a module is one which is closed under vector operations. This is a sufficient condition for the subset of vectors in the submodule to themselves form a module. ;)
(; //LEAN:Submodule ;)
Submodule : (R : Set) -> (M : Set) -> Set.

(; //LEAN:Submodule.module ;)
Submodule__module : (R : Set) -> (M : Set) -> (module_M : Proof (Module R M)) -> (p : Elem (Submodule R M)) -> Proof (Module R (Subtype M (x2 => Membership__mem M (Submodule R M) p x2))).

(; doc: The span of a set `s ⊆ M` is the smallest submodule of M that contains `s`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Submodule.span ;)
Submodule__span : (R : Set) -> (M : Set) -> (s : Elem (powerset M)) -> Elem (Submodule R M).

(; doc: Embedding of a submodule `p` to the ambient space `M`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Submodule.subtype ;)
Submodule__subtype : (R : Set) -> (M : Set) -> (module_M : Proof (Module R M)) -> (p : Elem (Submodule R M)) -> Elem (LinearMap R R (RingHom__id R) (Subtype M (x2 => Membership__mem M (Submodule R M) p x2)) M).

(; doc: A _subsingleton_ is a type with at most one element. It is either empty or has a unique element.  All propositions are subsingletons because of proof irrelevance: false propositions are empty, and all proofs of a true proposition are equal to one another. Some non-propositional types are also subsingletons. ;)
(; //LEAN:Subsingleton ;)
Subsingleton : (X : Set) -> Prop.

(; doc: If a type is a subsingleton, then all of its elements are equal. ;)
(; //LEAN:Subsingleton.elim ;)
Subsingleton__elim : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Subsingleton X)) -> (a : Elem X) -> (b : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt X a b).

(; doc: All the elements of a type that satisfy a predicate.  `Subtype p`, usually written `{ x : α // p x }` or `{ x // p x }`, contains all elements `x : α` for which `p x` is true. Its constructor is a pair of the value and the proof that it satisfies the predicate. In run-time code, `{ x : α // p x }` is represented identically to `α`.  There is a coercion from `{ x : α // p x }` to `α`, so elements of a subtype may be used where the underlying type is expected.  Examples:  * `{ n : Nat // n % 2 = 0 }` is the type of even numbers.  * `{ xs : Array String // xs.size = 5 }` is the type of arrays with five `String`s.  * Given `xs : List α`, `List { x : α // x ∈ xs }` is the type of lists in which all elements are    contained in `xs`.  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `{ x // p x }` in identifiers is `subtype`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Subtype ;)
Subtype : (X : Set) -> (p : (Elem X -> Prop)) -> Set.

(; //LEAN:Subtype.ext_iff ;)
Subtype__ext_iff : (X : Set) -> (p : (Elem X -> Prop)) -> (a1 : Elem (Subtype X (x4 => p x4))) -> (a2 : Elem (Subtype X (x3 => p x3))) -> Proof (iff (Eqt (Subtype X (x => p x)) a1 a2) (Eqt X (Subtype__val X (x1 => p x1) a1) (Subtype__val X (x2 => p x2) a2))).

(; //LEAN:Subtype.mk ;)
Subtype__mk : (X : Set) -> (p : (Elem X -> Prop)) -> (val : Elem X) -> (property : Proof (p val)) -> Elem (Subtype X (x => p x)).

(; doc: The value in the underlying type that satisfies the predicate. ;)
(; //LEAN:Subtype.val ;)
Subtype__val : (X : Set) -> (p : (Elem X -> Prop)) -> (self : Elem (Subtype X (x => p x))) -> Elem X.

(; doc: The top (`⊤`, `\top`) element  Conventions for notations in identifiers:   * The recommended spelling of `⊤` in identifiers is `top`. ;)
(; //LEAN:Top.top ;)
Top__top : (X : Set) -> Elem X.

(; doc: `True` is a proposition and has only an introduction rule, `True.intro : True`. In other words, `True` is simply true, and has a canonical proof, `True.intro` For more information: [Propositional Logic](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/propositions_and_proofs.html#propositional-logic) ;)
(; //LEAN:True ;)
True : Prop.

(; doc: Congruence in the function argument: if `a₁ = a₂` then `f a₁ = f a₂` for any (nondependent) function `f`. This is more powerful than it might look at first, because you can also use a lambda expression for `f` to prove that `<something containing a₁> = <something containing a₂>`. This function is used internally by tactics like `congr` and `simp` to apply equalities inside subterms.  For more information: [Equality](https://lean-lang.org/theorem_proving_in_lean4/quantifiers_and_equality.html#equality) ;)
(; //LEAN:congrArg ;)
congrArg : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (x : Elem X) -> (x1 : Elem X) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (h : Proof (Eqt X x x1)) -> Proof (Eqt Y (f x) (f x1)).

(; doc: Similar to `congrFun` but `β` does not depend on `α`. ;)
(; //LEAN:congrFun' ;)
congrFun' : (X : Set) -> (Y : Set) -> (f : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (g : (Elem X -> Y)) -> (h : Proof (Eqt (Elem X -> Y) f g)) -> (a : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt Y (f a) (g a)).

(; //LEAN:eq_false ;)
eq_false : (p : Prop) -> (h : Proof (not p)) -> Proof (Eqt Prop p false).

(; //LEAN:forall_congr ;)
forall_congr : (X : Set) -> (p : (Elem X -> Prop)) -> (q : (Elem X -> Prop)) -> (h : Proof (forall X (a2 => Eqt Prop (p a2) (q a2)))) -> Proof (Eqt Prop (forall X (a => p a)) (forall X (a1 => q a1))).

(; doc: The identity function. `id` takes an implicit argument `α : Sort u` (a type in any universe), and an argument `a : α`, and returns `a`.  Although this may look like a useless function, one application of the identity function is to explicitly put a type on an expression. If `e` has type `T`, and `T'` is definitionally equal to `T`, then `@id T' e` typechecks, and Lean knows that this expression has type `T'` rather than `T`. This can make a difference for typeclass inference, since `T` and `T'` may have different typeclass instances on them. `show T' from e` is sugar for an `@id T' e` expression. ;)
(; //LEAN:id ;)
id : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> Elem X.

(; //LEAN:imp_self._simp_1 ;)
imp_self___simp_1 : (a : Prop) -> Proof (Eqt Prop (if a a) True).

(; //LEAN:implies_congr ;)
implies_congr : (X : Set) -> (X1 : Set) -> (X2 : Set) -> (X3 : Set) -> (h : Proof (Eqt Set X X1)) -> (h1 : Proof (Eqt Set X2 X3)) -> Proof (Eqt Set (Elem X -> X2) (Elem X1 -> X3)).

(; //LEAN:implies_true ;)
implies_true : (X : Set) -> Proof (Eqt Prop (forall X (x => True)) True).

(; doc: A version of `le_refl` where the argument is implicit ;)
(; //LEAN:le_rfl ;)
le_rfl : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Preorder X)) -> (a : Elem X) -> Proof (Leq a a).

(; //LEAN:of_eq_true ;)
of_eq_true : (p : Prop) -> (h : Proof (Eqt Prop p True)) -> Proof p.

(; //LEAN:one_ne_zero ;)
one_ne_zero : (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (NeZero X 1)) -> Proof (Neq 1 0).

(; //LEAN:one_smul ;)
one_smul : (M : Set) -> (X : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Monoid M)) -> (_ : Proof (MulAction M X)) -> (b : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt X (HSMul__hSMul M X X 1 b) b).

(; //LEAN:or_false ;)
or_false : (p : Prop) -> Proof (Eqt Prop (or p false) p).

(; doc: `rfl : a = a` is the unique constructor of the equality type. This is the same as `Eq.refl` except that it takes `a` implicitly instead of explicitly.  This is a more powerful theorem than it may appear at first, because although the statement of the theorem is `a = a`, Lean will allow anything that is definitionally equal to that type. So, for instance, `2 + 2 = 4` is proven in Lean by `rfl`, because both sides are the same up to definitional equality. ;)
(; //LEAN:rfl ;)
rfl : (X : Set) -> (a : Elem X) -> Proof (Eqt X a a).

(; //LEAN:smul_eq_zero._simp_1 ;)
smul_eq_zero___simp_1 : (R : Set) -> (M : Set) -> (_ : Proof (Semiring R)) -> (_ : Proof (AddCommMonoid M)) -> (_ : Proof (Module R M)) -> (r : Elem R) -> (m : Elem M) -> (_ : Proof (Module__IsTorsionFree R M)) -> (_ : Proof (IsCancelMulZero R)) -> Proof (Eqt Prop (Eqt M (HSMul__hSMul R M M r m) 0) (or (Eqt R r 0) (Eqt M m 0))).

